1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,436
An independent label puts out a record.

2
00:00:02,476 --> 00:00:03,833
For anyone to hear it,

3
00:00:03,873 --> 00:00:08,405
somebody has to find the few hundred
people who might actually play it —

4
00:00:08,445 --> 00:00:10,627
programmers, curators, show hosts —

5
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:12,977
and write to each of them personally,

6
00:00:13,017 --> 00:00:14,160
with a real pitch.

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:16,929
That's weeks of work per release.

8
00:00:16,969 --> 00:00:18,566
Most labels never do it.

9
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:20,719
So most records just disappear.

10
00:00:21,219 --> 00:00:22,552
Spindle does it. Every night,

11
00:00:22,592 --> 00:00:24,059
for every artist on the roster.

12
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,453
Get it wrong and you're
not marketing, you're spam.

13
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:30,579
So the whole thing turns on
picking the right few hundred.

14
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:34,106
That shortlist is a vector
search in CockroachDB,

15
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:37,201
with the filters — tenant,
model, contactable —

16
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:39,242
living inside the index itself,

17
00:00:39,282 --> 00:00:40,600
not bolted on after.

18
00:00:41,079 --> 00:00:43,350
And that index starts with tenant_id.

19
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:47,700
One column doing our security boundary
and our partition key at once.

20
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:50,441
pgvector can do the similarity.

21
00:00:50,481 --> 00:00:52,740
It can't make one decision do both jobs.

22
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:54,962
The index is never finished, either.

23
00:00:55,002 --> 00:00:57,678
Agents are adding to
these records all day —

24
00:00:57,718 --> 00:00:59,776
what a station plays, who runs it,

25
00:00:59,816 --> 00:01:01,359
what they said last time.

26
00:01:01,719 --> 00:01:02,991
And there's no orchestrator.

27
00:01:03,031 --> 00:01:04,397
Agents don't call each other —

28
00:01:04,437 --> 00:01:06,920
a changefeed on the memory
tables wakes the next one.

29
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:09,035
So kill half the fleet mid-run.

30
00:01:09,075 --> 00:01:10,425
The work still finishes.

31
00:01:10,465 --> 00:01:12,840
The lease expires and the row comes back.

32
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:17,504
The send claims its lease before the
network call. Two workers, same name.

33
00:01:17,544 --> 00:01:20,120
The name can't tell them
apart. The token can.

34
00:01:20,599 --> 00:01:22,016
When people write back,

35
00:01:22,056 --> 00:01:24,043
the reply finds its own thread —

36
00:01:24,083 --> 00:01:27,819
every pitch carries a token we
minted for one conversation.

37
00:01:28,379 --> 00:01:31,078
We're holding contact
details for real people,

38
00:01:31,118 --> 00:01:34,591
and European law says where
that data can live. Same table,

39
00:01:34,632 --> 00:01:36,735
same query: this row is in Virginia,

40
00:01:36,775 --> 00:01:38,800
this one is physically in Ireland.

41
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,633
Residency per row, enforced by the
database instead of by us remembering to.

42
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:45,700
Now lose a region. It keeps answering.

43
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:49,330
It spends the label's money too,
on its own, up to a ceiling.

44
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:52,043
Last night it asked to put
more behind one record,

45
00:01:52,083 --> 00:01:53,060
and I approved it.

46
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:56,153
So this morning — what was
it looking at when it asked?

47
00:01:56,193 --> 00:01:57,326
I can't just query it.

48
00:01:57,366 --> 00:01:58,659
We never stop collecting,

49
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:02,700
so those metrics have been overwritten
and the thread states have moved on.

50
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,872
But the ledger row stored the exact
logical timestamp it decided at.

51
00:02:06,912 --> 00:02:08,238
Four extra words of SQL,

52
00:02:08,278 --> 00:02:09,719
pointed at that timestamp,

53
00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:11,979
and they come back. Not a copy of them.

54
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:16,530
Then it re-runs the valuation against those old numbers and checks it still gets the

55
00:02:16,569 --> 00:02:17,469
same answer.

56
00:02:17,719 --> 00:02:19,596
No audit table. No event log.

57
00:02:19,636 --> 00:02:21,620
No versioned copy of anything.

58
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,379
Postgres cannot do that at any price.

59
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:29,630
One database doing the index,
the scheduler, the outbox,

60
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:31,873
the event bus and the ledger.

61
00:02:31,913 --> 00:02:34,620
It scales to zero between releases.

62
00:02:35,159 --> 00:02:39,083
It's live today at
spindle.mattrickslauer.com.

63
00:02:39,123 --> 00:02:40,759
Go and make it big.
